      "role": "user",
      "content": "but thats the point though. they arent meant to be perfect engineering reflectors, they're meant to be \"natural\" covers. if you use a rigid metal dish, everyone knows there is a mic there. but if you use snake plants, most people just see \"decor\" and think nothing of it. even if the signal is slightly scattered, a modern AI-driven signal processing algorithm could probably clean up that audio and reconstruct the speech easily. isnt it true that sound reinforcement doesn't need a \"perfect\" reflection to work, just enough to boost the signal-to-noise ratio for a sensitive sensor?",
